; 07-no-top-level-values.l0 -- a declaration is a def or a data (spec 4),
; so a table computed once, such as CRC-32's 256 entries or SHA-256's 64
; round constants, cannot be named at the top level. A def with no
; parameters is recomputed at every call, so the table is built once and
; then passed as a parameter to every function that reads it: ../hash/sums.l0
; carries a Tables value through nine functions, and bits.l0's xor and and
; tables through every call that splits a word. Passing is free (spec 10.3,
; read-only parameters), so the cost is in signatures, not time.
; What would have been written:
(def crc_table (array int) (array_new 256 0))

(def crc_step ((crc int) (b int)) int
  (array_get crc_table (mod (add crc b) 256)))

(def main () int (crc_step 0 97))
